      {
        "id": "4Mx40OzVGUuP3zRU",
        "title": "Text 1",
        "content": {
          "type": "Text",
          "format": "html",
          "text": [
            "<h2><strong>Why does a computer need examples in order to learn to recognize earthquakes?</strong></h2><p>A computer can’t simply &quot;find earthquakes.&quot; Humans need to decide what data to provide, identify examples, determine what patterns matter.  Let’s look at seismic data from Anchorage. Can you find earthquakes?</p>"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "d6mPDWDZpZoaEkv5",
        "title": "Text 3",
        "content": {
          "type": "Text",
          "format": "html",
          "text": [
            "<p><strong>Instructions</strong></p><p>1) In the WaveRunner tile under Challenge 2, Click on the box that says “Choose a station” scroll through the list and choose one seismograph. </p><p>2) Click on the box that says “Choose a model” and click on “compact model.”</p><p>3) Type in a date range.</p><p>4) Click the &quot;Timeline It!&quot; button  (the button furthest to the right)in the bottom control bar of the WaveRunner tile. The Timeline tile will display the data for the station and dates you selected.</p><p>5) Use the Timeline tile to search through the seismic data for signals of earthquakes. Be sure to check that each signal you choose matches the shape of the ones above!</p><p>6) When you have found a signal, record data its characteristics in the table.</p><p></p>"
          ]
        }
      },
